// Grow lights light the plots near them (2026-09-26, gardening depth).
// Read by src/systems/farming/lighting.rs, which carries the model; this file
// carries the numbers and where each came from. Sources read on 2026-09-26.
//
// THE MODEL. A powered grow light puts out PPF = watts x ppe_umol_per_j
// micromoles of photons a second. Each hour it lights a plot counts as an hour
// of sun if it holds the density of an average sunlit hour: the reference
// daily light integral (DLI) over the daylight seconds (24 h x the daylight
// share, farming::DAYLIGHT_FRACTION, 0.5 = 43,200 s). The area one light covers
// at that density is PPF / it; a plot whose crops need more than the reference
// (plants.csv dli_target) takes proportionally more canopy of the same light.
// It covers the plots nearest it first, within reach_m, and a plot it only
// partly covers grows that share. Two lights over one bed add up. The lights
// run on a timer (lamp_photoperiod_h) from sunset, so a night is not all lit.
// Outdoor fields get only the sun.
//
// A 100 W light: 100 x 2.30 = 230 umol/s over 17e6 / 43,200 = 394 umol/m2/s
// covers 0.58 m2 of a crop needing 17. Run through a whole 12 h night that
// would be 1.2 kWh for 0.58 m2, 2.1 kWh per m2 a day, which agrees with the
// 2.2 kWh per m2 a day for greens in docs/design/self-sufficiency.md, worked
// out independently. The 18 h timer runs it 6 h: 0.6 kWh, and half a day of
// extra growth.
//
// SOURCES
//
// ppe_umol_per_j: DesignLights Consortium, "Technical Requirements for
//   LED-based Horticultural Lighting, Version 3.0" (2024 PDF,
//   https://designlights.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/DLC_HORT-Technical-Requirements-V3-0_FINAL_05132024.pdf,
//   summarised at https://designlights.org/our-work/horticultural-lighting/technical-requirements/hort-v3-0/):
//   "The Hort V3.0 PPE threshold has been increased to a minimum of
//   2.30 umol x J-1". The minimum a listed LED grow light may have, so a
//   plain listed fixture; better ones reach about 3.
//
// target_dli_mol_m2_d: Cornell University CEA Program, "Hydroponic Lettuce
//   Handbook" (2013), Lighting section:
//   https://cpb-us-e1.wpmucdn.com/blogs.cornell.edu/dist/8/8824/files/2019/06/Cornell-CEA-Lettuce-Handbook-.pdf
//   "It should be noted that 17 mol/m2/d is the light integral that worked
//   best for the particular cultivar of boston bibb lettuce that we used."
//   Now the REFERENCE a light's area is quoted at; each crop's own need is its
//   plants.csv dli_target (see PER-CROP NEED below). Some lettuces tip-burn
//   above about 15 (the same page).
//
// cup_canopy_m2: the same handbook, pond growing area: "After re-spacing (from
//   97 plants m-2 to 38 plants m-2)". 1 / 38 = 0.026 m2 of canopy a finished
//   plant needs, used for each planted cup of a tower.
//
// lamp_photoperiod_h: the timer. Lights switch on at sunset and off once the
//   day's light reaches this many hours, so 18 leaves six hours of dark.
//   Runkle (Michigan State), "Technically speaking: Lighting greenhouse
//   vegetables", Greenhouse Product News, December 2011, p. 42,
//   https://www.canr.msu.edu/uploads/resources/pdfs/lightingvegetables.pdf
//   (read 2026-09-26 through the Internet Archive): "Some crops, especially
//   tomato, become stressed and develop chlorotic leaves if grown under
//   continuous light. Therefore, four to six hours of darkness is suggested
//   each night." Some potato cultivars are also intolerant of continuous light
//   (Wheeler et al., in docs/reference/findings/2026-09-26-crop-daily-light-integrals.md).
//   So the light model's "second day of growth" from a lamp is now six hours
//   of it, half a day, not the whole night.
//
// PER-CROP NEED (2026-09-26): target_dli_mol_m2_d is now the REFERENCE a
//   light's covered area is quoted at. A plot's own need is the highest
//   plants.csv dli_target among its crops (sourced for 27 crops in the findings
//   document above), and it takes need / 17 times the canopy of the same light;
//   a crop without a sourced target is planned at 17 and its card says so.
//
// reach_m: GAME ESTIMATE. A fixture hangs over the bed it serves; light
//   falls off with the square of distance, so a bed a few metres away gets a
//   negligible share. Plots whose centre is farther than this, measured
//   across the floor, get none of the light.
(
    ppe_umol_per_j: 2.30,
    target_dli_mol_m2_d: 17.0,
    cup_canopy_m2: 0.026,
    reach_m: 3.0,
    lamp_photoperiod_h: 18.0,
)
